1
00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:04.170
In this lesson, you dive into
the techniques of reordering documents

2
00:00:04.371 --> 00:00:07.607
to improve information
retrieval relevance and quality.

3
00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:11.544
You'll learn
how to use specific metadata values

4
00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:14.481
to determine reordering position.

5
00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:16.816
Let's go.

6
00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:20.453
There are scenarios
where a document can contain other fields

7
00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:23.723
that affect its position
within such results.

8
00:00:24.224 --> 00:00:29.224
Take, for example, an Airbnb listing
with rating and number of reviews

9
00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:29.896
field.

10
00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:33.666
This fields indicates
qualitative and quantitative measures

11
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:37.170
that can contribute to the relevance
of a document with respect

12
00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.173
to a user query and search criteria.

13
00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:44.010
Taken into consideration,
the value of these fields in order

14
00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:47.547
to affect the position of a document
in the list of return

15
00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:50.950
search
results, is referred to as boosting.

16
00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:55.388
Why should you consider adding a boosting
technique in your search queries?

17
00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:59.893
Vector search is an effective
method of ranking documents

18
00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:04.431
based on semantic similarity.
Although vector search scores and ranking

19
00:01:04.431 --> 00:01:08.334
effective, metadata values
can contribute to the document

20
00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:12.305
relevance, which can affect the ordering
within search results.

21
00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:16.109
Using additional qualitative
and quantitative measures

22
00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:20.680
to rank documents and shows database
operation results are credible

23
00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:24.717
and relevant to user queries
and their search criteria.

24
00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:29.918
Boosting can also be used
to make sure results meet user

25
00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:35.023
specific requirements, which introduce
personalization within search results.

26
00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:40.266
In the coding section, you're
going to go through some familiar steps.

27
00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:45.738
The first would be to set up a rank pipeline,
and you will add the relevant stages.

28
00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:49.742
Then, you add a boosting logic

29
00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:53.513
which will use some mathematical operators

30
00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:56.749
available within MongoDB database.

31
00:01:57.083 --> 00:02:01.387
And as usual, you handle the user
query and visualize results.

32
00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:04.157
Let's code.

33
00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:07.393
Start by importing your custom
utils module

34
00:02:07.494 --> 00:02:09.762
like you've done in the previous lesson.

35
00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:11.831
Move on to load the data.

36
00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:14.467
Also,
like you've done in previous lessons.

37
00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:19.172
You can also take some time to view
the attributes of each data points.

38
00:02:19.372 --> 00:02:22.909
Move on to the document modeling
which loads the listing

39
00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:26.112
into a conformed model.

40
00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:29.816
This is similar to process that you've
carried out in previous lessons.

41
00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:34.988
The next step is to get an object
of your database and your collection.

42
00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:38.858
Then start of a clean collection
by calling the delete

43
00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:40.627
any on the collection record.

44
00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:43.029
This is similar to the process
that has been carried out

45
00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:44.497
in previous lessons.

46
00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:47.734
Go through the data ingestion process
and move on

47
00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:50.770
to the vector
search index definition process.

48
00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.473
All similar to the previous lesson.

49
00:02:53.473 --> 00:02:57.277
Now, you define a search result item model

50
00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.513
for the results shown in this lesson.

51
00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:04.651
The attributes for each results
needs to contain a combined score,

52
00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:08.188
number of reviews, and average review
scores.

53
00:03:08.388 --> 00:03:11.391
These new attributes will be explained
later.

54
00:03:11.758 --> 00:03:15.595
Again, just like in the previous lesson,
you have to handle user

55
00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:18.565
query function with the exact same code.

56
00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:22.135
Now, we can get to the main aspects
of this lesson.

57
00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:26.239
You'll be implementing a boosting logic
and adding it

58
00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:30.710
to the vector search operations
conducted on the aggregation pipeline.

59
00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:33.680
Here, you are assigning

60
00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.150
to a variable named "review average stage".

61
00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:41.888
In this cell,
what is happening is, we are adding

62
00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:46.921
two new fields to every document returned
from the database operation.

63
00:03:47.193 --> 00:03:50.263
The first field is the average
review score.

64
00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.765
Now, this is a qualitative measure

65
00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:55.768
I was talking about. The average review
score

66
00:03:55.802 --> 00:03:59.839
is going to go through
every review component of a document

67
00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:05.278
and take an average of a sum of the review
components.

68
00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:08.948
So within every document
we can see the accuracy,

69
00:04:09.148 --> 00:04:13.987
the cleanliness, the check
in and other attributes of a listing.

70
00:04:14.354 --> 00:04:19.354
Get the score
and with the dollar operator, specifically

71
00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:24.330
the dollar add which conducts
the mathematical operation of an addition,

72
00:04:24.631 --> 00:04:29.102
we can get a sum
of all the review component,

73
00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:32.305
and then we can divide it

74
00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:36.709
by the number of review components,

75
00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:41.014
which in this case
for the listings in our data set six.

76
00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:46.247
This gives us an idea of what the average
rating of a listing is.

77
00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:49.789
That explains the new field
that we're adding

78
00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:52.959
to every document
called the average review score.

79
00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:58.426
The second field that has been added to
every document is the review count boost.

80
00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:03.036
This is a quantity measure,

81
00:05:03.036 --> 00:05:06.039
and this field will take the value

82
00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:09.976
of the number of reviews attributes
in each document.

83
00:05:11.044 --> 00:05:15.081
This is how you can pass
the value of one field to a new field.

84
00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:18.818
Simply using the dollar operator
and the name of the field.

85
00:05:19.852 --> 00:05:24.724
To add this new field to every document
in the database operation,

86
00:05:25.291 --> 00:05:29.162
you can add this process as a new stage,
specifically

87
00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:32.265
the add fields stage.

88
00:05:32.265 --> 00:05:37.036
That concludes adding the qualitative
measure and the quantitative measure.

89
00:05:37.603 --> 00:05:40.873
In the next step, you'll need to add

90
00:05:41.107 --> 00:05:46.107
weights and determine how each component
of the quantitative measure

91
00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:50.416
and the quantitative measure
should affect the ranking

92
00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:53.453
of a document after a vector

93
00:05:53.453 --> 00:05:55.154
search operation.

94
00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:59.225
This is done
by adding a new stage to our pipeline.

95
00:05:59.992 --> 00:06:01.861
This is the weighting stage.

96
00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:05.531
Now the weighting stage comes right
after the review

97
00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:07.166
average stage.

98
00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:11.204
So, the weighting stage will
then have a reference

99
00:06:11.471 --> 00:06:15.241
of the average review score
and the review count boost

100
00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:20.113
that was added to each document
in the review average stage.

101
00:06:20.847 --> 00:06:23.116
This is how you can reference

102
00:06:23.116 --> 00:06:26.786
the values of these fields
from the documents.

103
00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:29.789
To implement a weighting logic,

104
00:06:30.857 --> 00:06:32.992
you will use several operators

105
00:06:32.992 --> 00:06:37.263
enabled by MongoDB database
to conduct mathematical operation.

106
00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:40.833
The add operator
and the multiply operator.

107
00:06:40.933 --> 00:06:44.971
For the multiply operator,
you will multiply the value

108
00:06:44.971 --> 00:06:49.275
of the average review score, which
is the qualitative measure by a weight.

109
00:06:49.642 --> 00:06:54.642
I'm using the number between 0
and 1 to assign a weight.

110
00:06:55.515 --> 00:06:58.818
Then do the same for the review

111
00:06:58.818 --> 00:07:01.821
count boost,
which is a quantitative measure

112
00:07:02.255 --> 00:07:06.592
that will be considered
to rank the document after the vector

113
00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:07.927
search operation.

114
00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:11.898
You then use the add operator
to combine the two results

115
00:07:11.998 --> 00:07:15.802
from the different multiplication
operations.

116
00:07:16.102 --> 00:07:19.272
Assign this new additional value

117
00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:22.642
to the field combined score.

118
00:07:22.842 --> 00:07:27.180
The combined score is the combination
of the two multiplied value,

119
00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:32.084
and we can add the combined score
to each document

120
00:07:32.084 --> 00:07:37.084
within the database operation
by using the add fields operator.

121
00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:40.359
This is the weighting stage.

122
00:07:40.359 --> 00:07:43.362
There is one more stage
to complete this process.

123
00:07:43.796 --> 00:07:46.799
The final stage is the sorting stage.

124
00:07:47.066 --> 00:07:49.101
The sorting stage is very simple.

125
00:07:49.101 --> 00:07:54.101
Using the dollar operator sort,
we can actually rerank the documents

126
00:07:55.975 --> 00:08:00.346
based on their combined score
or a certain field.

127
00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.449
In this case,
you are using the combined score

128
00:08:03.449 --> 00:08:06.419
and you are reranking it in
descending order.

129
00:08:06.419 --> 00:08:09.422
So, this is indicated by minus one

130
00:08:09.989 --> 00:08:12.992
ascending
order will be indicated by a one.

131
00:08:13.292 --> 00:08:15.828
Now that you have
all the additional stages

132
00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:20.828
implemented to add to the vector search
operation, you can create a new variable

133
00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:26.400
called Additional Stages, that takes a list
of all the defined stages.

134
00:08:27.507 --> 00:08:29.942
The first is the review stage,

135
00:08:29.942 --> 00:08:34.942
where we conduct a mathematical operation
to gain the qualitative

136
00:08:35.915 --> 00:08:40.915
and quantitative measure and add it
as a new field to the documents

137
00:08:41.087 --> 00:08:44.624
After the vector search operation.
Then there is a weighting stage,

138
00:08:44.991 --> 00:08:49.362
and then there is a sorting stage.
All the stages are executed

139
00:08:49.362 --> 00:08:53.065
sequentially
after the vector search operation.

140
00:08:54.133 --> 00:08:56.702
Remember, the vector search operation

141
00:08:56.702 --> 00:09:01.702
we're using in this lesson, is a pre-filter
in vector search.

142
00:09:02.141 --> 00:09:05.444
Similar to the ones
you've created in previous lessons.

143
00:09:06.913 --> 00:09:08.581
Now it's time for you

144
00:09:08.581 --> 00:09:11.651
to see the results of the boosting logic.

145
00:09:13.085 --> 00:09:17.990
Using the same query from previous lessons
and also the same function

146
00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:22.295
from previous lesson, the handle user
query function, you will pass

147
00:09:22.295 --> 00:09:26.198
in the additional stage
and make note to use

148
00:09:26.198 --> 00:09:29.201
the vector index with filter.

149
00:09:29.235 --> 00:09:33.706
Here, you can observe that
the vector search operation stage

150
00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:37.243
was conducted
in a fraction of a millisecond.

151
00:09:37.643 --> 00:09:42.643
Now, let's observe the documents
that were returned from this operation

152
00:09:43.115 --> 00:09:48.115
that included a combination of stages
to simulate a boosting logic.

153
00:09:49.055 --> 00:09:51.257
Here you can see the results

154
00:09:51.257 --> 00:09:54.727
of the database operation
that included multiple stages.

155
00:09:55.061 --> 00:09:59.298
The average review score is included
along with the numbers of reviews

156
00:09:59.298 --> 00:10:00.933
and the combined score.

157
00:10:00.933 --> 00:10:05.933
Remember, the combined score includes
a weightage consideration.

158
00:10:06.238 --> 00:10:11.238
Now, the documents shown are ordered
by the combined score.

159
00:10:11.978 --> 00:10:16.949
You can pause the video here and observe
the combined score of the other documents.

160
00:10:17.583 --> 00:10:21.420
One thing to note, is that because of the
weighting logic we added,

161
00:10:22.555 --> 00:10:24.323
you will observe

162
00:10:24.323 --> 00:10:27.627
that despite this document,
having a high rating,

163
00:10:28.461 --> 00:10:32.431
it is ranked lower in comparison
to the other documents above it

164
00:10:32.698 --> 00:10:35.968
because it had a lower number of reviews.

165
00:10:36.369 --> 00:10:39.038
This is the impact of adding weights

166
00:10:39.038 --> 00:10:42.074
to the components you're considering
for your boosting and logic.

167
00:10:42.608 --> 00:10:43.409
One more thing.

168
00:10:43.409 --> 00:10:47.079
You can play with the weights
and adjust the numbers

169
00:10:47.613 --> 00:10:50.616
to see how it affects the results.

170
00:10:51.017 --> 00:10:55.221
To do this, simply
go back to the weighting stage

171
00:10:55.721 --> 00:10:58.457
and adjust the weights.

172
00:10:58.457 --> 00:11:02.261
Now I'm giving higher weights
to the review count

173
00:11:02.695 --> 00:11:05.698
and a lower one to the average reviews.

174
00:11:12.104 --> 00:11:14.440
Once you've changed the weights,

175
00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:17.443
you can observe the results again.

176
00:11:23.082 --> 00:11:25.217
As you can observe from the results,

177
00:11:25.217 --> 00:11:28.487
because you've added more weightage
to the number of reviews

178
00:11:28.821 --> 00:11:32.058
a document
with a high number of reviews is ranked

179
00:11:32.058 --> 00:11:35.594
higher than one
with a high number of rating

180
00:11:36.162 --> 00:11:37.663
in comparison.

181
00:11:37.663 --> 00:11:40.132
Pause
the video here to observe the results.

182
00:11:41.367 --> 00:11:44.470
In this lesson, you've learned
how to implement

183
00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:48.607
a typical RAG system,
conduct vector search.

184
00:11:49.075 --> 00:11:54.075
But now, you've added multiple stages
to the aggregation pipeline

185
00:11:55.614 --> 00:11:58.617
to simulate a boost in logic,

186
00:11:59.185 --> 00:12:02.388
which adds more relevance and context

187
00:12:02.688 --> 00:12:06.192
to the ranking of your documents

188
00:12:06.358 --> 00:12:08.594
after a database operation.

189
